Market Challenges
Deploying monitoring sensors across extensive overhead networks requires reliable communications, consistent data quality, and compatibility with existing utility systems. Distinguishing normal environmental fluctuations from genuine electrical or structural faults can also be technically difficult.

Utilities must evaluate false alarms, device performance, installation requirements, and integration with outage-management and field-dispatch workflows. These considerations can complicate procurement and slow large-scale deployment.

Market Restraints
Coverage economics and communications reliability are major restraints. Monitoring large numbers of geographically dispersed poles can require substantial installation and maintenance resources, while weak connectivity may limit the usefulness of real-time alerts.

A shortage of qualified technical personnel, restricted site access, integration delays, and pricing pressure can further affect implementation. Utilities may therefore adopt phased deployments rather than immediately extending monitoring across entire networks.

